§ `Anti-social Behaviour, Crime and Policing Act 2014 — section 43`

-- The P4b showcase: a module with NO `DECLARE` at all, whose entire ontology
-- comes from top-level `ASSUME`. Adapted from `jl4/examples/legal/anti-social.l4`
-- (which is NOT modified).
--
-- WHAT THIS SEED IS FOR. Before the ASSUME→RInput widening, `L4.Relational.Lower`
-- bailed on any reference to an `ASSUME`d name, so an ASSUME-shaped module had
-- no Blawx image at all and `interviewTest` recovered an input's category and
-- arity from the record-FIELD tables — meaning a module with no records got no
-- interview test either (the P1 earmark, left open on `sumlist`). This file is
-- the discharge of that earmark: five `ASSUME`d TYPEs become Blawx categories,
-- nine `ASSUME`d predicates become the declared attributes that hang off them,
-- and the emitted `interview` test abduces every one of them.
--
-- THE ORIGINAL'S SPELLING CANNOT BE USED, and the reason is not in this leg.
-- `jl4/examples/legal/anti-social.l4` writes every input as
-- `ASSUME `is authorised` IS A FUNCTION FROM Person TO BOOLEAN`. Lowering is
-- export-rooted, and `L4.Export.validateExportInputs` makes it a TYPE ERROR for
-- an `@export`ed decision to reference a function-typed `ASSUME`
-- ("Function type inputs are not supported for @export") because it appends
-- referenced `ASSUME`s to the export's parameter list and a function cannot
-- cross the web app's JSON boundary. So a module in that spelling has no export
-- root and never reaches the middle end at all. The original's own comment at
-- `:30-33` records the same wall from the other side: it says the decision is
-- deliberately NOT `@export`ed.
--
-- The fix is a re-spelling, not a change of meaning: `GIVEN p IS A Person`
-- + `ASSUME `is authorised` p IS A BOOLEAN` declares the same one-place
-- predicate with a non-arrow declared type, and passes. The form is
-- long-standing L4 (`jl4/examples/ok/signatures.l4:9-10`), and the middle end
-- admits BOTH spellings, so nothing here has to move if the export rule is
-- later relaxed. Whether it should be — an `ASSUME`d predicate is a perfectly
-- good logic-program input and a nonsensical web form field — is reported as a
-- question against `L4.Export`, not decided here.
--
-- TWO TYPES ARE RENAMED, and the reason is a hard Blawx error, not taste.
-- The original has both a TYPE `Conduct` and an accessor `conduct`, and both a
-- TYPE `Effect` and an accessor `effect`. Measured on this tree, `l4 check`
-- accepts that and `l4 blawx` refuses it:
--
--     in `conduct`: name collision (Blawx): distinct L4 definitions `Conduct`
--     and `conduct` both mangle to the Blawx atom `conduct` — rename one
--
-- The accessors keep the Act's words, because that is where they read as the
-- statute and because a value attribute's synthesised NLG is "has <name> of";
-- the TYPEs become `Behaviour` and `Consequence`. Recorded as a deviation.
--
-- `EffectTarget` is carried over from the original UNREFERENCED, on purpose: it
-- is the corpus witness for the reachability gate. An `ASSUME` no lowered
-- clause reaches must contribute nothing — otherwise a module that merely
-- carries one would gain a predicate and move some other backend's golden — so
-- `effect_target` must NOT appear anywhere in the goldens beside this file.
--
-- THE ORACLE PROBLEM, AND HOW IT IS SOLVED HERE. `ASSUME` is uninterpreted, so
-- `l4 run` cannot answer for a decision in this file, and `lowerQuery` builds a
-- scenario only out of a record-literal query argument — so an `#EVAL` here
-- would lower to a query with no facts at all. This file therefore carries NO
-- directives, and its `.blawx` carries only the `interview` test. Pairing zero
-- oracles against zero tests would be a VACUOUS green, which is worse than a
-- failure because it looks like coverage.
--
-- The anchor is `antisocial-twin.l4`: the same logic spelled with `DECLARE`
-- records whose field names are these `ASSUME` names character for character,
-- so the two files lower to the SAME s(CASP) atoms at the same arities. The
-- twin's `#EVAL`s DO run under `l4 run`, and `etc/blawx-tier1-harness.py` uses
-- the twin's measured value as the single expectation for BOTH programs: it
-- replays the twin's fact rows against THIS file's workspaces and requires the
-- same answer. One logic, two spellings, cross-checked — no expectation is
-- authored twice, and none is hand-derived.
--
-- THE GROUPING, MEASURED. The original's body mixes AND and OR across four
-- indentation levels, which is the same "read the layout" hazard as
-- `rodents.l4`. Measured over eleven scenarios on a record twin carrying the
-- original's exact AND/OR columns, the parse is
--
--     authorised AND (individual-16-or-over OR body)
--                AND (detrimental AND persistent AND affects-quality)
--                AND unreasonable
--
-- The two discriminating rows — "not authorised but a body, everything else
-- true" and "authorised individual with none of the conduct limbs" — both
-- evaluate FALSE, which rules out every reading that lets the `OR` escape its
-- conjunct. Both rows are kept in the twin as regression pins. The seed writes
-- the grouping with parentheses so the reading is on the page rather than in
-- the indentation.
--
-- EXPORT ORDER IS AUTHORING, NOT LAYOUT: one `interview` test is emitted per
-- file and its goal is the FIRST `@export`, so the headline decision comes
-- first.
--
-- The `@ref` on `the conduct is unreasonable` is deliberate and carries no
-- prose: it is the corpus witness for the `rule_text` fallback
-- `@desc -> @ref -> stub`, whose middle arm no committed golden exercised
-- before. Read its section in `expected/antisocial.blawx`: it should be the
-- citation, not "Definition of the conduct is unreasonable."

ASSUME Person IS A TYPE
ASSUME Receiver IS A TYPE
ASSUME Behaviour IS A TYPE
ASSUME Consequence IS A TYPE

-- Unreferenced on purpose: the reachability gate's corpus witness (see header).
ASSUME EffectTarget IS A TYPE

GIVEN p IS A Person
ASSUME `is authorised` p IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN r IS A Receiver
ASSUME `is an individual aged 16 or over` r IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN r IS A Receiver
ASSUME `is a body` r IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN r IS A Receiver
ASSUME conduct r IS A Behaviour

GIVEN b IS A Behaviour
ASSUME effect b IS A Consequence

GIVEN b IS A Behaviour
ASSUME `is unreasonable` b IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN c IS A Consequence
ASSUME `is detrimental` c IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN c IS A Consequence
ASSUME `is of a persistent or continuing nature` c IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN c IS A Consequence
ASSUME `affects the quality of life of those in the locality` c IS A BOOLEAN

@export Anti-social Behaviour, Crime and Policing Act 2014 s.43(1): an authorised person may issue a community protection notice to an individual aged 16 or over, or to a body, if satisfied on reasonable grounds that (a) the conduct of the individual or body is having a detrimental effect, of a persistent or continuing nature, on the quality of life of those in the locality, and (b) the conduct is unreasonable.
@ref https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2014/12/section/43
GIVEN p IS A Person
      r IS A Receiver
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `may issue a community protection notice` IF
        `is authorised` p
    AND `the recipient is a person to whom a notice may be given` r
    AND `the conduct is having a detrimental effect of a persistent or continuing nature` r
    AND `the conduct is unreasonable` r

@export s.43(1): the notice may be given "to an individual aged 16 or over, or to a body". The two limbs are alternatives, and nothing else is a permissible recipient.
GIVEN r IS A Receiver
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the recipient is a person to whom a notice may be given` IF
        `is an individual aged 16 or over` r
     OR `is a body` r

@export s.43(1)(a): the conduct of the individual or body "is having a detrimental effect, of a persistent or continuing nature, on the quality of life of those in the locality".
GIVEN r IS A Receiver
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the conduct is having a detrimental effect of a persistent or continuing nature` IF
    `the effect is detrimental, persistent, and affects the quality of life in the locality` (effect (conduct r))

-- The three limbs of s.43(1)(a) are properties of ONE effect, and this decision
-- is what makes that so in the emitted logic program. Written inline over
-- `effect (conduct r)` three times, ANF gives each occurrence its own variable
-- (`effect(C1,E1), … effect(C2,E2), …`), because an ASSUMEd value function
-- lowers to a RELATION with no functionality constraint. Under a scenario that
-- states one effect per conduct the two encodings agree; under the interview's
-- abduction they do not, and a model could satisfy each limb with a DIFFERENT
-- effect. Taking the effect as a parameter binds it once and removes the
-- question. (The divergence is reported as a finding against the widening: an
-- ASSUMEd function is single-valued in L4 and multi-valued in s(CASP).)
@export s.43(1)(a), of the effect itself: it must be "detrimental", "of a persistent or continuing nature", and on "the quality of life of those in the locality" — all three, of the same effect.
GIVEN c IS A Consequence
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the effect is detrimental, persistent, and affects the quality of life in the locality` IF
        `is detrimental` c
    AND `is of a persistent or continuing nature` c
    AND `affects the quality of life of those in the locality` c

@export
@ref Anti-social Behaviour, Crime and Policing Act 2014, s.43(1)(b)
GIVEN r IS A Receiver
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the conduct is unreasonable` IF
    `is unreasonable` (conduct r)
